  <title>8.2.4: Solving a Real-World Problem Using Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02111</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.2.4: Solving a Real-World Problem Using Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2ef0c017-502d-4dab-b002-09f05f9e4efc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>A movie theater models the revenue from ticket sales in one day as a function of the ticket price, \(p\). Here are two expressions defining the same revenue function.</para>
<para>\(p(120 − 4p)\)</para>
<para>\(120p − 4p^2\)</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>According to this model, how high would the ticket price have to be for the theater to make $0 in revenue? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the ticket price and your reasoning.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>$30. If \(p(120 − 4p) = 0\), then either \(p = 0\) or \(120 − 4p = 0\). If the latter is a true equation, \(p\) must be 30.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What equation can you write to find out what ticket price(s) would allow the theater to make $600 in revenue?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter an equation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(p(120−4p) = 600\) or \(120p − 4p^2 = 600\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
